### Clarify
Assume a singly linked list, no cycle, and that mutating `Next` pointers is allowed. Return `null` for an empty list. If the input may be cyclic or must remain unchanged, that changes the contract.

### Approach
Walk once with `previous` and `current`. Save `current.Next` before rewiring it; otherwise the unvisited suffix is lost. After the loop, `previous` is the new head.

```csharp
public sealed class ListNode
{
    public int Value;
    public ListNode? Next;

    public ListNode(int value, ListNode? next = null)
    {
        Value = value;
        Next = next;
    }
}

public static ListNode? ReverseList(ListNode? head)
{
    ListNode? previous = null;
    ListNode? current = head;

    while (current is not null)
    {
        ListNode? next = current.Next;
        current.Next = previous;
        previous = current;
        current = next;
    }

    return previous;
}
```

### Complexity
`O(n)` time and `O(1)` auxiliary space. A recursive solution is also `O(n)` time but uses `O(n)` call-stack space.

### Edge cases
Empty and one-node lists work without special cases. For a cyclic list, first reject or break the cycle; this loop would otherwise never terminate.

### Clarify
Return the length, not the substring. The source prompt guarantees ASCII and contiguous characters; assume a non-null input. For arbitrary Unicode, clarify whether uniqueness means UTF-16 code units, Unicode scalars, or grapheme clusters.

### Approach
Maintain a sliding window `[left, right]` with no duplicate. `lastSeen[c]` stores the most recent index of each ASCII character. When `c` repeats, move `left` to at least one position after its prior index; never move `left` backward.

```csharp
public static int LongestSubstringWithoutRepeat(string s)
{
    ArgumentNullException.ThrowIfNull(s);

    var lastSeen = new int[128];
    Array.Fill(lastSeen, -1);
    int left = 0;
    int best = 0;

    for (int right = 0; right < s.Length; right++)
    {
        char c = s[right];
        if (c >= 128)
            throw new ArgumentException("ASCII input required.", nameof(s));

        left = Math.Max(left, lastSeen[c] + 1);
        lastSeen[c] = right;
        best = Math.Max(best, right - left + 1);
    }

    return best;
}
```

### Complexity
`O(n)` time because each character is processed once, and `O(1)` space for the fixed 128-entry table. With a dictionary for an unbounded alphabet, space is `O(min(n, alphabet size))`.

### Edge cases
`""` returns `0`; all-equal input returns `1`; a fully unique string returns its length. The `Math.Max` is essential for cases such as `abba`, where an old duplicate lies left of the current window.

### Clarify
The critical ambiguity is precedence. I would define: rules are active on `[ValidFrom, ValidUntil)`; the nearest node with any active rule for the user overrides ancestors; `Deny` wins over `Allow` when both occur on that same node; no matching rule means deny. Confirm whether groups, actions such as read/write, and symlinks are in scope.

### Approach
Start at the file and walk toward the root. At each level, evaluate only active rules for the user. Return immediately once that level has an answer, because it is the most specific level. Track node IDs defensively to reject a malformed cyclic hierarchy.

```csharp
public enum Effect { Allow, Deny }

public sealed record PermissionRule(
    string UserId,
    Effect Effect,
    DateTimeOffset ValidFrom,
    DateTimeOffset? ValidUntil);

public sealed record FsNode(
    string Id,
    FsNode? Parent,
    IReadOnlyList<PermissionRule> Rules);

public static bool HasAccess(
    FsNode file,
    string userId,
    DateTimeOffset at)
{
    var visited = new HashSet<string>(StringComparer.Ordinal);

    for (FsNode? node = file; node is not null; node = node.Parent)
    {
        if (!visited.Add(node.Id))
            throw new InvalidOperationException(
                "Filesystem hierarchy contains a cycle.");

        var active = node.Rules.Where(r =>
            r.UserId == userId &&
            r.ValidFrom <= at &&
            (r.ValidUntil is null || at < r.ValidUntil));

        bool found = false;
        bool allow = false;

        foreach (var rule in active)
        {
            found = true;
            if (rule.Effect == Effect.Deny)
                return false;

            allow = true;
        }

        if (found)
            return allow;
    }

    return false;
}
```

### Complexity
`O(h + r)` time for hierarchy height `h` and rules examined `r`, with `O(h)` defensive cycle-detection space. If rules are indexed by user per node and the tree is trusted, lookup approaches `O(h)` time and `O(1)` extra space.

### Edge cases
Test exact start/end boundaries, conflicting rules at one level, expired child overrides revealing an ancestor rule, missing users, root files, and malformed parent cycles. In a real filesystem, resolve and canonicalize the target safely and define symlink behavior to prevent path traversal from bypassing the policy.

### Clarify
Define duplicates as byte-for-byte identical, not merely equal names. Confirm whether to recurse, follow symbolic links, filter by one extension, include empty files, and report hard links as separate duplicates. A safe default is recursive scan, case-insensitive extension filter, skip links or reparse points, include empty files, and continue past inaccessible paths.

### Approach
Use a staged pipeline: enumerate regular files, group by size, optionally hash a small prefix for very large groups, then stream a full SHA-256 only for remaining candidates. For adversarial or compliance-sensitive input, byte-compare equal hashes before reporting. This avoids reading uniquely sized files.

```csharp
using System.Security.Cryptography;

public static IReadOnlyList<IReadOnlyList<string>> FindDuplicates(
    string root,
    string? extension = null)
{
    var options = new EnumerationOptions
    {
        RecurseSubdirectories = true,
        IgnoreInaccessible = true,
        AttributesToSkip = FileAttributes.ReparsePoint
    };

    var sameSizeGroups = Directory.EnumerateFiles(root, "*", options)
        .Where(p => extension is null ||
            string.Equals(
                Path.GetExtension(p),
                extension,
                StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase))
        .Select(p => new FileInfo(p))
        .Where(f => f.Exists)
        .GroupBy(f => f.Length)
        .Where(g => g.Count() > 1);

    var result = new List<IReadOnlyList<string>>();

    foreach (var sameSize in sameSizeGroups)
    {
        foreach (var sameHash in sameSize.GroupBy(
                     f => Sha256(f.FullName),
                     StringComparer.Ordinal))
        {
            if (sameHash.Count() > 1)
                result.Add(sameHash.Select(f => f.FullName).ToArray());
        }
    }

    return result;
}

static string Sha256(string path)
{
    using var stream = new FileStream(
        path,
        FileMode.Open,
        FileAccess.Read,
        FileShare.Read,
        1 << 20,
        FileOptions.SequentialScan);

    return Convert.ToHexString(SHA256.HashData(stream));
}
```

### Complexity
Let `F` be files discovered and `B` bytes read from candidate files. Time is `O(F + B)` and grouping metadata is `O(F)`. Streaming bounds per-file memory; the hash tables still scale with the number of candidates.

### Edge cases and production hardening
Files can change between stat and hash: record size and modification time before reading, re-stat afterward, and retry or mark unstable. Catch and report per-file I/O failures rather than aborting the scan. Bound hashing concurrency to avoid saturating disk, support cancellation, normalize extension input, and compare file identity if hard links should count once. For millions of files, spill metadata to a database or external sort instead of retaining all entries in memory.

### Clarify
Assume the transcript supplies sorted, non-overlapping cues with `[start, end)` timestamps, and highlighting is per cue or word. The audio element's playback clock is authoritative. Clarify how to display gaps, overlapping timestamps, live transcripts, seeks, and playback-rate changes.

### Approach
Validate and sort cues once. On play, run a `requestAnimationFrame` loop and read `audio.currentTime`; on pause or end, stop it; on seek, recompute immediately. Find the latest cue whose start is not after the position via binary search, then verify its end. Update the DOM only when the cue index changes. Never advance an independent timer—the media clock already accounts for buffering, seeks, and playback rate.

```csharp
public sealed record Cue(
    TimeSpan Start,
    TimeSpan End,
    string Text);

public static int FindActiveCue(
    IReadOnlyList<Cue> cues,
    TimeSpan position)
{
    int low = 0;
    int high = cues.Count - 1;
    int candidate = -1;

    while (low <= high)
    {
        int mid = low + (high - low) / 2;

        if (cues[mid].Start <= position)
        {
            candidate = mid;
            low = mid + 1;
        }
        else
        {
            high = mid - 1;
        }
    }

    return candidate >= 0 && position < cues[candidate].End
        ? candidate
        : -1;
}
```

Browser-side orchestration is small but important:

```text
on play: frameId = requestAnimationFrame(tick)
on pause/end: cancelAnimationFrame(frameId)
on seeking/seeked: render(findCue(audio.currentTime))
tick: find cue from audio.currentTime; render only if changed; schedule next frame
```

### Complexity
Preprocessing is `O(n log n)` if sorting is needed, otherwise `O(n)` validation. Each lookup is `O(log n)` and cue storage is `O(n)`. During uninterrupted playback, an index cursor can make normal forward progression amortized `O(1)`, while binary search remains the fallback for seeks.

### Edge cases
Return no highlight in timestamp gaps. Reject negative or inverted intervals. Define overlap precedence—this search chooses the latest-starting cue. Recompute after backward seeks, handle zero cues and end-of-media, escape transcript text, and expose the active state accessibly with `aria-current` or an equivalent. For streaming transcripts, append only monotonic cues or reindex when late corrections arrive.

### Clarify
Assume the input maps each task to its prerequisites, every referenced prerequisite is also a task, and a valid answer may be any topological order. Although the prompt says DAG, production code should still detect a cycle. Duplicate dependency edges should not inflate indegrees.

### Approach
Use Kahn's algorithm. Build edges from each prerequisite to the tasks that depend on it and count each task's remaining prerequisites. Repeatedly emit a zero-indegree task and decrement its dependents. A sorted ready set makes the output deterministic.

```csharp
public static IReadOnlyList<string> TopologicalOrder(
    IReadOnlyDictionary<string, IReadOnlyCollection<string>> prerequisites)
{
    var nodes = new HashSet<string>(prerequisites.Keys);
    foreach (var deps in prerequisites.Values)
        nodes.UnionWith(deps);

    var indegree = nodes.ToDictionary(n => n, _ => 0);
    var dependents = nodes.ToDictionary(n => n, _ => new List<string>());

    foreach (var (node, deps) in prerequisites)
    {
        foreach (string dependency in deps.Distinct())
        {
            dependents[dependency].Add(node);
            indegree[node]++;
        }
    }

    var ready = new SortedSet<string>(
        nodes.Where(n => indegree[n] == 0),
        StringComparer.Ordinal);

    var order = new List<string>(nodes.Count);

    while (ready.Count > 0)
    {
        string node = ready.Min!;
        ready.Remove(node);
        order.Add(node);

        foreach (string dependent in dependents[node])
        {
            if (--indegree[dependent] == 0)
                ready.Add(dependent);
        }
    }

    if (order.Count != nodes.Count)
    {
        var blocked = nodes.Where(n => indegree[n] > 0);
        throw new InvalidOperationException(
            $"Dependency cycle detected among: {string.Join(", ", blocked)}");
    }

    return order;
}
```

### Complexity
With a FIFO queue, time is `O(V + E)` and space is `O(V + E)`. The sorted ready set used for deterministic output changes time to `O((V + E) log V)` in the worst case.

### Edge cases
Handle an empty graph, disconnected components, tasks that appear only as prerequisites, duplicate edges, self-dependencies, and cycles. For parallel execution, the ready set represents runnable tasks, but mark a dependent ready only after all prerequisites complete successfully; define failure propagation, retries, concurrency limits, and cancellation separately from topological ordering.

### Clarify
Assume the array may be modified in place and contains only `0` and `1`. Because equal values are indistinguishable, stability is irrelevant. Decide whether invalid values should be rejected; this implementation validates them while scanning.

### Approach
Use two pointers. Advance `left` past zeroes and `right` past ones. When `left` points to `1` and `right` points to `0`, swap them. Every iteration either advances a pointer or performs a swap and advances both.

```csharp
public static void SortBinaryArray(int[] values)
{
    ArgumentNullException.ThrowIfNull(values);

    int left = 0;
    int right = values.Length - 1;

    while (left <= right)
    {
        if (values[left] is not (0 or 1) ||
            values[right] is not (0 or 1))
        {
            throw new ArgumentException(
                "Array must contain only 0 and 1.",
                nameof(values));
        }

        if (values[left] == 0)
        {
            left++;
        }
        else if (values[right] == 1)
        {
            right--;
        }
        else
        {
            (values[left], values[right]) =
                (values[right], values[left]);
            left++;
            right--;
        }
    }
}
```

### Complexity
`O(n)` time and `O(1)` auxiliary space. A counting solution is also `O(n)` time and `O(1)` space and may be simpler: count zeroes, then overwrite the prefix with zeroes and the suffix with ones. The two-pointer version can perform fewer writes when the input is mostly sorted.

### Edge cases
Empty, one-element, all-zero, all-one, already sorted, and reverse-grouped arrays all work. If validation must inspect every element even after pointers cross, validate in a separate pass; with the loop above, each element is encountered by one of the pointers before termination.
